<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olof Skötkonung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 980–1022</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olof Skötkonung was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from about 995 and the first Swedish king known to have been a Christian, traditionally baptised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husaby">Husaby</a>. Son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-the-victorious">Eric the Victorious</a>, he ruled both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svear">Svear</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a>, a union regarded as the beginning of the medieval Kingdom of Sweden, and minted the first Swedish coins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigtuna">Sigtuna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olof-skotkonung.jpg" alt="Olof Skötkonung" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown 11th century mintmaster; photo/scan by unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olaf_Scotking_of_Sweden_coin_c_1030.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Olof Skötkonung, sometimes called Olaf the Swede, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-the-victorious">Eric the Victorious</a> and, according to Icelandic sources, of Sigrid the Haughty. He is the first Swedish ruler about whom there is substantial knowledge, though knowledge of his life rests mostly on the accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>, which some scholars criticise. His epithet Skötkonung has been explained as meaning tributary king, or from the Swedish skatt, taxes or treasure, or from the ancient ceremony of scotting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> had been expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-the-victorious">Eric the Victorious</a>, and that when Eric died, Sweyn returned and married his widow. Olof then attacked and expelled Sweyn, but the conflict was settled and the two kings became allies. Around 1000 Olof joined Sweyn and the Norwegian jarls Eric and Sven against the Norwegian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-svolder">Battle of Svolder</a>. Olaf Tryggvason disappeared during the fight, and the victors divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>: Olof gained part of Trøndelag and the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf II of Norway</a> re-established the Norwegian kingdom in 1015, war broke out again. In 1018 Olof's cousin Ragnvald Ulfsson urged him at the thing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> to make peace and marry his daughter Ingegerd to the Norwegian king. Olof instead married Ingegerd to Yaroslav I the Wise, and his other daughter Astrid went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and married Olaf II. The Swedes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> were displeased with his self-willed rule, and he agreed to share power with his son Anund Jacob.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Olof was the first king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> known to have minted coins, and a few hundred survive as contemporary evidence for his reign. They were struck at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigtuna">Sigtuna</a> in imitation of English pennies; the earliest call him King in Sigtuna and the later ones King of the Swedes, which may reflect a wider base of power around 1000.</p><p>He was the first Swedish king to remain a Christian until his death. A Westrogothic law of about 1240 says he was baptised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husaby">Husaby</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland">Västergötland</a> by the missionary Sigfrid, though the circumstances are unclear. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> says he planned to tear down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> temple but had to give up the plan because many Swedes were still pagan, and he installed a bishopric in Västergötland instead. His reign is considered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> to mark the transition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olof Skötkonung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Olof Skötkonung</b> (r. c. 995–c. 1022), the first Christian king, was probably the first to rule both Svear and Geats, and he struck the first Swedish coins at Sigtuna.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In a large naval battle in the western Baltic, King Olaf Tryggvason of Norway was defeated and killed by an alliance of Sweyn Forkbeard of Denmark, <b>Olof Skötkonung</b> of Sweden and Eric Haakonsson, Earl of Lade.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-9">9 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1000 the Geats came under the kings of the Svear, and <b>Olof Skötkonung</b>, traditionally baptised at Husaby in Västergötland, ruled both peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-geats">Kingdom of the Geats</a></li><li><blockquote>Eric the Victorious, who defeated a rival at Fýrisvellir in the 980s, and his son <b>Olof Skötkonung</b> extended Svear rule over the Geats.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-swedes">Kingdom of the Swedes</a></li></ul></section>
